window.__finlexDocLoaded(10186,{"id":10186,"articles":[{"id":1000775,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Governs classification of debt (callable/refinanced obligations), simplified convertible debt accounting without cash-conversion/CCF bifurcation (ASC 470-20, ASU 2020-06), and debt modifications/extinguishments (ASC 470-50, 10% cash-flow test).","seq":0},{"id":1000776,"no":"ASC 470-10 / 470-20 / 470-50","heading":"Convertible Debt (ASU 2020-06) & 10% Modification Test","text":"• Convertible Debt (ASC 470-20, ASU 2020-06): Eliminated beneficial conversion feature (BCF) and cash conversion separation models — most convertible debt is now accounted for as a SINGLE liability at amortized cost (unless embedded derivative bifurcation applies under ASC 815).\n• Debt Modifications (ASC 470-50-40-10): Substantially different terms (>= 10% change in PV of cash flows) trigger extinguishment accounting (gain/loss in P&L); < 10% is accounted for prospectively via a new effective interest rate (no immediate P&L gain/loss!).\n• Debt Issuance Costs (ASC 835-30): Presented as a direct deduction from the carrying amount of the debt liability.","seq":1},{"id":1000777,"no":"US GAAP vs. IFRS / CAS","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Differences","text":"• Convertible Debt: IAS 32 ALWAYS requires split accounting (liability + equity component) for compound convertible bonds; ASU 2020-06 accounts for most US GAAP convertible bonds as a single liability.\n• Non-Substantial Modification (<10%): IFRS 9.5.4.3 requires an IMMEDIATE P&L gain/loss (recalculating PV at original EIR); ASC 470-50 amortizes the difference prospectively with NO Day-1 P&L gain/loss.","seq":2},{"id":1000778,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":3}]});
